1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,873
Inspection rounds happen in basements and pump rooms, often with no signal.

2
00:00:06,923 --> 00:00:10,912
Rounds brings AI to the technician there,

3
00:00:10,962 --> 00:00:14,950
without letting AI decide what is safe.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,877
A supervisor starts with the equipment manual.

5
00:00:18,927 --> 00:00:23,926
Firebase AI Logic sends it once to Gemini in

6
00:00:23,976 --> 00:00:28,974
the cloud, which compiles it into a procedure pack:

7
00:00:29,024 --> 00:00:33,462
typed limits the app can enforce, and one

8
00:00:33,512 --> 00:00:37,950
small card per step for the local model.

9
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,350
Every limit and safety rule must quote the manual

10
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:49,350
word for word, and the app checks this in code.

11
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:57,150
Here it flagged a sentence the AI reworded, so the supervisor excludes it.

12
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:01,950
Nothing reaches technicians until each item is approved.

13
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,728
The technician starts the round.

14
00:01:04,778 --> 00:01:10,839
The approved pack, with its limits and quotes, is now stored

15
00:01:10,889 --> 00:01:16,950
on the device, so the whole round works without a network.

16
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,950
Now the connection drops, as it often does down here.

17
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,132
The technician describes a squeal near the bearing.

18
00:01:26,182 --> 00:01:31,882
Chrome's built-in Gemini Nano, called through the same Firebase AI Logic

19
00:01:31,932 --> 00:01:37,632
SDK, reviews the note against this step, on the device, offline.

20
00:01:37,682 --> 00:01:41,291
It suggests what the note is missing.

21
00:01:41,341 --> 00:01:44,950
It is a suggestion, never a decision.

22
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,217
Then an abnormal reading:

23
00:01:47,267 --> 00:01:48,350
82 degrees.

24
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:51,183
No AI judges this number.

25
00:01:51,233 --> 00:01:56,283
A rule compares it with the approved limit, shows

26
00:01:56,333 --> 00:02:01,950
the manual's sentence, and the escalation rule the supervisor approved.

27
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,656
For a deeper look, the technician sees

28
00:02:05,706 --> 00:02:09,362
exactly what would be sent, and agrees.

29
00:02:09,412 --> 00:02:14,656
While offline, it waits in a queue on the device,

30
00:02:14,706 --> 00:02:19,950
with an ID that stops it from being sent twice.

31
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:26,157
When the connection returns, the review runs in the cloud,

32
00:02:26,207 --> 00:02:32,984
with the manual section and this check's history from earlier shifts.

33
00:02:33,034 --> 00:02:37,950
The ledger records where every call really ran.

34
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:43,260
At the end of the shift, the device drafts the handover.

35
00:02:43,310 --> 00:02:47,605
The facts underneath come from the recorded readings, and

36
00:02:47,655 --> 00:02:51,950
the open issues carry over to the next technician.

37
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:52,450
Rounds.

38
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:57,950
Cloud for the manual, device for the field, rules for safety.
